Context
    DocumentVersion: 0.1.0
    GrammarVersion: 1.0.1
    Language: en

Page
    Type: Page
    Role: Content
    Purpose: Concept of the Semantic Text Format (STF) core — why it exists, what it is, and how it relates to application vocabularies such as SIL.

    Introduction
        Type: Section
        Role: Content
        Caption: 1. Introduction

        Text
            Content: The Semantic Text Format (STF) is a text format for semantic representation. It was born alongside the Semantic Interface Layer (SIL) specification, as the concrete syntax that SIL needed in order to describe interactive applications to intelligent agents. During the development of SIL, it became clear that the format itself — the syntax, the object model, and the structural trust boundary — was more fundamental than its first application. STF is that format, separated from any single use case.

        Text
            Content: A web page for a human is HTML, CSS, JavaScript. The browser turns them into a visual interface. An STF document is a textual semantic description of structure and meaning — readable by a human, and directly interpretable by a language model without task-specific training, fine-tuning, or system prompts. At the same time it is a formal grammar that a machine can parse deterministically.

    Origin
        Type: Section
        Role: Content
        Caption: 2. Where STF Came From

        Text
            Content: STF was created while implementing SIL on the AIS Platform website. SIL needed a way to describe an application's interface, navigation, interaction model, and capabilities directly to an agent. The format that resulted proved general: it expressed not just UI, but any structured semantic content. The decision was therefore made to extract the format into its own project — the STF core — and let SIL become one application vocabulary on top of it.

        Text
            Content: This document and the STF Specification are themselves written in STF. The standard and the example are one: STF is self-hosting.

    Purpose
        Type: Section
        Role: Content
        Caption: 3. Purpose of STF

        Text
            Content: STF enables structured information to be expressed in a form that is both human-readable and machine-parseable, optimized for direct consumption by intelligent agents. Its objective is not to replace existing technologies (JSON, XML, YAML, HTML) but to complement them with a semantic layer that is unambiguous by structure and safe by construction.

        Text
            Content: The primary objective can be expressed in a single sentence: enable information to describe itself in a form an intelligent agent can understand directly, without reverse-engineering meaning from presentation or behavior.

    Vision
        Type: Section
        Role: Content
        Caption: 4. Vision

        Text
            Content: STF is based on the idea that structured meaning should be expressible as plain text that an agent can read the way a human reads an outline. Indentation shows hierarchy. PascalCase names show structure. Colon-separated properties carry values. No brackets, no quotation marks for keys, no escaping. The format should be so transparent that the structure is obvious at a glance and yet rigid enough that a parser can reject any malformed input without guessing intent.

        Text
            Content: The long-term vision is that semantic text documents become a natural way for software to communicate structure and intent — not only to agents, but between systems and people. Future software should not merely be usable by agents through scraping and inference; it should be understandable by them through explicit communication.

    Scope
        Type: Section
        Role: Content
        Caption: 5. Scope

        Text
            Content: STF is intentionally independent of implementation technologies. It is not tied to any programming language, framework, transport protocol, serialization format, AI model, operating system, or browser. It is also independent of any application domain: UI, configuration, documentation, knowledge representation, and agent memory are all valid uses.

        Text
            Content: The STF core deliberately defines no transport and no application vocabulary. Those are supplied by extensions (see the Specification, §5). SIL is one such extension; others are expected.

    WhatSTFIs
        Type: Section
        Role: Content
        Caption: 6. What STF Is

        Text
            Content: STF today comprises:

        Bullet
            Type: Text
            Caption: Syntax
            Content: A concrete syntax defined by a formal EBNF grammar. Indentation-based (4 spaces), PascalCase object names, colon-separated properties. A model reads it like a human reads an outline.

        Bullet
            Type: Text
            Caption: Object Model
            Content: Every node has an ObjectName, a Type, a Role, and optionally an Id. Properties are classified as Data, Guidance, or Control. Meaning is explicit; inference is minimized.

        Bullet
            Type: Text
            Caption: Trust Model
            Content: A structural security model. The trust boundary is enforced by the grammar and type system, not by filtering strings. Origin, Knowledge Zones, and a privilege lattice make untrusted content data by construction.

        Bullet
            Type: Text
            Caption: Extension Mechanism
            Content: Vendor properties (reverse-domain prefix) and application vocabularies let any domain add Types, Roles, Actions, and transports without redefining core semantics.

    DesignPhilosophy
        Type: Section
        Role: Content
        Caption: 7. Design Philosophy

        Text
            Content: STF does not attempt to teach agents how to use software. Instead, it enables software (and any structured content) to explain itself. Understanding emerges from explicit communication, not from observation and reconstruction. The role of STF is communication, not visualization.

    FundamentalPrinciples
        Type: Section
        Role: Content
        Caption: 8. Fundamental Principles

        Text
            Content: Every feature in the format supports these principles. If a proposed extension conflicts with them, the principles take precedence.

        Principle
            Type: Text
            Caption: 8.1 Self-Description
            Content: Content should be able to describe itself. An agent should not be required to reconstruct information that already exists in the structure.

        Principle
            Type: Text
            Caption: 8.2 Semantic First
            Content: STF describes meaning before presentation. Visual appearance belongs to a different layer; STF communicates concepts.

        Principle
            Type: Text
            Caption: 8.3 Deterministic Interpretation
            Content: STF eliminates ambiguity where structure can carry meaning. The grammar, object model, and trust model are unambiguous and machine-parseable by design. Natural-language fields (Purpose, Description) remain probabilistic — STF scopes them clearly so agents know what is structural and what is advisory.

        Principle
            Type: Text
            Caption: 8.4 Structural Security
            Content: The trust boundary is enforced by the grammar and type system, not by filtering dangerous-looking strings. UserContent is data by construction. Knowledge Zones separate trusted system content from untrusted visitor content. The Origin system tracks provenance with a fail-closed default (Unknown = Untrusted).

        Principle
            Type: Text
            Caption: 8.5 Extensibility
            Content: No format can anticipate every use case. STF provides a stable semantic core while allowing application-defined extensions and vendor properties. Extensions MUST NOT redefine core semantics.

        Principle
            Type: Text
            Caption: 8.6 Technology Neutrality
            Content: STF must not depend on any specific programming language, framework, transport protocol, serialization format, AI model, operating system, or browser. The format describes semantics, not implementation.

        Principle
            Type: Text
            Caption: 8.7 Agent-Centric Reading
            Content: STF is designed so that an intelligent agent can consume it directly. Every structural choice favors explicit, low-ambiguity communication over brevity won through convention.

    ThreeLayerArchitecture
        Type: Section
        Role: Content
        Caption: 9. The Three-Layer Architecture

        Text
            Content: STF is defined as three layers, only the first two of which belong to the core. The third is an application concern.

        Layer
            Type: Section
            Role: Content
            Caption: Layer 1 — STF Syntax
            Purpose: How to write and read. Text format, grammar, parsing rules. A parser validates syntax without knowing what "Button" means. This layer is the STF core.

        Layer
            Type: Section
            Role: Content
            Caption: Layer 2 — Semantic Vocabulary (core)
            Purpose: What it means. Types, Roles, States, Actions, Origin. The core object model and trust model are standardized; applications extend them. A server routes an action without knowing what Expand does to a Menu. This layer is the STF core.

        Layer
            Type: Section
            Role: Content
            Caption: Layer 3 — Application Protocol (extension)
            Purpose: How to interact. Transport, sessions, action submission, event delivery, discovery. This layer is defined by an application vocabulary (for example SIL over HTTP). It is NOT part of the STF core; the core only reserves the structural space for it.

        Text
            Content: Separation of concerns: a parser validates STF without knowing semantics. A consumer interprets capabilities without knowing implementation details. A different transport can use the same syntax and vocabulary — only the protocol layer changes.

    ThoughtExperiment
        Type: Section
        Role: Content
        Caption: 10. A Thought Experiment

        Text
            Content: Imagine an intelligent agent receiving a document for the first time. No prior knowledge. No screenshots. No training.

        Text
            Content: The agent's first questions are simple: What is this? What can I do here? What can I not do? Which actions are available? Which require authorization? What happens after each action?

        Text
            Content: Every major concept in STF answers one of these questions. If a concept does not improve an agent's (or a system's) understanding of the content, it does not belong in the core format.

    STFIsNot
        Type: Section
        Role: Content
        Caption: 11. STF Is Not

        Text
            Content: Defining what STF is not is as important as defining what it is.

        Text
            Content: STF is NOT a programming language or scripting environment. It is NOT a replacement for JSON, XML, or YAML as a data serialization format — it is optimized for semantic readability by agents, not for arbitrary machine-to-machine data. It is NOT a visual interface, an automation protocol, a testing framework, a page description language, or an AI prompt format. It does not define authentication, sessions, or transport. Those are application vocabularies built on top of STF.

    ConceptualArchitecture
        Type: Section
        Role: Content
        Caption: 12. Conceptual Architecture

        Text
            Content: STF sits between raw data and a consumer. A producer turns an application model or knowledge source into an STF document; a consumer reads structure and meaning directly. Neither the producer's internals nor the consumer's reasoning are part of the format. Both depend only on the STF document.

        Text
            Content: Application Model or Knowledge Source  ->  STF Document (core)  ->  Intelligent Agent or System. The format is the contract; the layers above and below it are free to vary.

    CoreSemanticModelInPractice
        Type: Section
        Role: Content
        Caption: 13. Core Semantic Model in Practice

        Text
            Content: STF defines the following core concepts. The table illustrates them through what they enable.

        ConceptTable
            Type: Section
            Role: Content
            Caption: Core concepts

            Row
                Type: Text
                Caption: Object
                Content: A named structural unit with Type, Role, properties, children. What is this?

            Row
                Type: Text
                Caption: Type / Role
                Content: Structural classification and semantic purpose. What kind of thing, and why does it exist?

            Row
                Type: Text
                Caption: Id
                Content: Stable addressable identifier. Which object do I act on?

            Row
                Type: Text
                Caption: State
                Content: Current state. What condition is it in?

            Row
                Type: Text
                Caption: Actions
                Content: Declared available interactions. What can I do?

            Row
                Type: Text
                Caption: Origin
                Content: Data provenance. Can I trust it?

            Row
                Type: Text
                Caption: Knowledge Zones
                Content: Is this system content or visitor content?

            Row
                Type: Text
                Caption: Expects
                Content: What parameters does this action require?

        Text
            Content: Each concept exists because a consumer needs to know it. An application vocabulary adds concrete values (Button, Expand, ProductCard) without changing these core roles.

    Example
        Type: Section
        Role: Content
        Caption: 14. Example

        Text
            Content: A minimal STF document illustrating the core structure:

        Example
            Type: Text
            Caption: Minimal STF document
            Content: "Context backslash-n    DocumentVersion: 0.1.0 backslash-n    GrammarVersion: 1.0.1 backslash-n    Language: en backslash-n backslash-n Page backslash-n    Type: Page backslash-n    Role: Content backslash-n    Section backslash-n        Type: Section backslash-n        Role: Content backslash-n        Text backslash-n            Type: Text backslash-n            Content: Hello, semantic world."

    FutureEvolution
        Type: Section
        Role: Content
        Caption: 15. Future Evolution

        Text
            Content: STF is a published format in its early stages. The syntax, core object model, and trust model are intended to be stable. Planned for future versions:

        Bullet
            Type: Text
            Caption: Streamable STF
            Content: Progressive rendering, incremental updates for very large documents.

        Bullet
            Type: Text
            Caption: Cross-document fragment identifiers
            Content: Addressing an object across documents by fragment.

        Bullet
            Type: Text
            Caption: Binary STF encoding
            Content: A compact encoding for minimal token usage in agent contexts.

        Bullet
            Type: Text
            Caption: Formal conformance test suite
            Content: Machine-readable tests for parsers and validators.

        Text
            Content: The format is developed in the open. Application vocabularies (such as SIL) evolve on their own timelines; the STF core evolves separately and conservatively.

    GuidingPrinciple
        Type: Section
        Role: Content
        Caption: 16. Guiding Principle

        Text
            Content: Every architectural decision within STF should be evaluated against one fundamental question:

        Quote
            Type: Text
            Caption: Criterion
            Content: Does this make structured meaning easier for an intelligent agent to understand?

        Text
            Content: If yes — the proposal aligns with the purpose of STF. If no — reconsider, regardless of technical elegance.

    Conclusion
        Type: Section
        Role: Content
        Caption: 17. Conclusion

        Text
            Content: For decades, structured data has been designed for machines to process and prose for humans to read. The emergence of intelligent agents introduces a participant that fits neither category cleanly: it reads text, but reasons over structure. STF is a format built for that participant — plain text that is unambiguous by grammar and safe by construction.

        Text
            Content: STF introduces no new business logic and changes no application behavior. It enables structured content to express what it already knows about itself in a form that intelligent agents can understand directly. It is an open format, ready for the community to adopt, implement, and extend — with SIL as its first, and furthest-developed, application vocabulary.
